\begin_body

\begin_layout Chapter
Design
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This chapter provides a comprehensive overview of the architectural and
 functional aspects of our RISC-V processor design.
 It details the structure of the processor’s pipeline and explains how various
 functional units are seamlessly integrated into the core.
 The flow of instruction execution—from fetching to write-back—is described
 along with how the pipeline handles different scenarios.
 The processor is equipped with full interrupt and exception handling, which
 has been carefully designed and integrated into the pipeline control logic.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A key enhancement in our design is the implementation of a Memory Management
 Unit 
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "MMU"
description "Memory Management Unit"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 that supports the Sv32 virtual memory scheme with a two-level page table
 structure and dynamic 
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "TLB"
description "Translation Lookaside Buffer"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 Translation Lookaside Buffer updates.
 To enforce memory access control, a Memory Protection Unit 
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "PMP"
description "Physical Memory Protection"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 has been incorporated that allows selective access to predefined physical
 memory regions, configurable during the boot process.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Additionally, a 
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "CLINT"
description "Core Local Interrupt controller"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 Core Local Interrupt controller has been integrated to manage software-generate
d and timer-based interrupts.
 For debugging, a comprehensive Debug Unit is developed that supports features
 such as single-stepping, hardware breakpoints, and the ability to inspect
 core registers and memory.
 This unit is interfaced with a custom-designed JTAG 
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "JTAG"
description "Joint Test Action Group"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 controller, enabling external debugging tools like GDB to connect and interact
 with the core during software development and testing.
\end_layout

