\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Instruction Fetch (IF
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "IF"
description "Instruction Fetch unit"
literal "true"

\end_inset

)
\series default
: The Instruction Fetch stage is responsible for retrieving instructions
 from memory using a tightly coupled 
\emph on
ICACHE
\emph default
 unit.
 Instructions are aligned on 32-bit word boundary and 
\series bold
fetch one instruction per cycle
\series default
, assuming the instruction-side memory system (e.g., instruction cache) is
 ready.
 The 
\emph on
inst_cache_stall
\emph default
 signal may be asserted in this stage when the instruction cache cannot
 immediately provide the requested data, resulting in a stall across the
 pipeline.
 Control signals like 
\emph on
kill_if
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
halt_if_id
\emph default
 can be asserted here to invalidate or stall instructions, typically due
 to branch mispredictions, exceptions, or interactions with the debug unit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\noindent
\align left

\series bold
Instruction Decode (ID
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "ID"
description "Instruction Decode unit"
literal "true"

\end_inset

)
\series default
: The Instruction Decode stage interprets the opcode and operands from the
 fetched instruction and initiates register file reads for source operands.
 This stage also interacts with the forwarding logic to resolve data hazards
 by reusing results from later stages when needed.
 If dependencies cannot be bypassed, stalls such as 
\emph on
halt_id_ie
\emph default
 may be introduced.
 Control and exception-related operations (e.g., CSR accesses) are decoded
 here, and 
\emph on
kill_id
\emph default
 can invalidate instructions due to misprediction or pipeline flushes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Execute (EX
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "EX"
description "Execute Unit"
literal "true"

\end_inset

)
\series default
: The Execute stage performs the actual computation required by the instruction.
 It houses key functional units including:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The Multiplier-Divider Unit (MDU) for integer multiply/divide instructions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\noindent
\align left
Branch conditions are evaluated in this stage, and branch decisions are
 made here.
 Multi-cycle operations—such as multiplication, division, and certain floating-p
oint operations—will stall the EX stage using control signals like 
\emph on
mult_div_stall
\emph default
 or 
\emph on
FPU_stall
\emph default
 until the operation completes.
 Data dependencies may also trigger pipeline stalls if forwarding is insufficien
t, particularly when dealing with memory or floating-point results.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Memory Access (MEM
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "MEM"
description "Memory Unit"
literal "true"

\end_inset

)
\series default
: In the Memory Access stage, load and store instructions interact with
 the data cache or memory-mapped I/O peripherals.
 Address values are passed from the EX stage, and the memory hierarchy returns
 or receives data accordingly.
 This stage handles stalls due to:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Double load/store hazards (e.g., 
\emph on
double_load_store_stall
\emph default
)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Data cache unavailability (
\emph on
dcache_stall
\emph default
)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Integration with the PMA/PMP unit ensures that memory accesses respect protectio
n and region-based access policies.
 The exception unit is actively involved here in validating memory accesses
 and raising faults if necessary.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Write-Back (WB
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "WB"
description "Write Back"
literal "true"

\end_inset

)
\series default
: The Write-Back stage completes the execution cycle by writing the results
 of ALU operations, memory loads, multiplication/division outputs, and floating-
point computations back into the register file.
 This stage marks the end of the instruction’s lifetime in the pipeline
 and also supports interaction with the debug module if a single-step or
 halt condition was in effect.
 The 
\emph on
halt_mem_wb signal
\emph default
 may pause this stage if there is an unresolved pipeline dependency, debug
 request, or flush event from exception or trap logic.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Control and Coordination Units
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Forwarding and Hazard Unit
\series default
: Ensures data is forwarded between pipeline stages when possible and detects
 data hazards that require stalls or data forwarding.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Exception Unit
\series default
: Detects illegal instructions and synchronous faults, and generates appropriate
 trap/interrupt control.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
CSR Logic
\series default
: Manages privileged state and communication with system-level software,
 integrating closely with the exception unit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Debug Unit
\series default
: Introduces halts, breakpoints, or kill signals to support single-stepping,
 abstract command execution, and visibility into processor state.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Kill and Halt Control Signals
\series default
: To resolve pipeline hazards or invalid instruction flows, the following
 kill and halt signals are used:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Kill Signals
\series default
: 
\emph on
kill_if
\emph default
, 
\emph on
kill_id
\emph default
, 
\emph on
kill_ie
\emph default
 Used to invalidate instructions in various stages (e.g.
 after a branch misprediction or exception).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Halt Signals:
\series default
 
\emph on
halt_if_id
\emph default
, 
\emph on
halt_id_ie
\emph default
, 
\emph on
halt_ie_mem
\emph default
, 
\emph on
halt_mem_wb
\emph default
 temporarily stop progression between stages to prevent incorrect execution
 or allow time for dependent data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\emph on
PC_CONTROL_IRQ
\emph default
, 
\emph on
PC_FREEZE_IRQ
\emph default
, 
\emph on
ID_IE_FLUSH
\emph default
 signals are asserted by the exception unit to halt the IF stage for fetching
 instructions from the ISR address and let other stages to complete the
 current instruction currently in the pipeline.
 This ensures less interrupt overhead.
 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
