\begin_layout Section
EXCEPTION UNIT
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The exception unit in RV32G core is responsible for managing and handling
 exceptions and interrupts according to the RISCV Privilaged specifications
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "RISCV_PRIV"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 It interfaces with various components of the processor, including the pipeline,
 CSR (Control and Status Register) block, and memory units.
 The primary functions of the exception unit include:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Detecting and handling synchronous and asynchronous exceptions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Updating relevant CSRs to reflect the state of the processor during an exception
 or interrupt.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Ensuring proper handling and resolution of exceptions to maintain system
 stability and security.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It handles the exception and interrupts for the Core.
 In the RISC-V architecture, exceptions and interrupts are both considered
 traps, but they differ fundamentally in their source and behavior.
 Exceptions are synchronous events that arise due to the execution of specific
 instructions—such as 
\series bold
\emph on
illegal operations, memory access faults, or system calls
\series default
\emph default
—and they occur predictably during the instruction flow.
 These are typically used to signal errors or specific conditions that require
 immediate attention by the processor.
 
\series bold
Interrupts
\series default
, on the other hand, are asynchronous events triggered by external or internal
 devices such as 
\series bold
timers, I/O peripherals, or software signals
\series default
.
 They occur independently of the current instruction execution and can happen
 at any point between instructions.
 While exceptions help the processor respond to internal execution issues,
 interrupts allow it to handle real-time events and interact with the outside
 world efficiently.
 Each of these exceptions have a specific code in the 
\series bold
\emph on
mcause
\series default
\emph default
 register in the CSR unit.
 When any of these occurs, 
\series bold
\emph on
mcause
\series default
\emph default
 register is loaded with the value assigned to the corresponding exception,
 current PC is stored in 
\series bold
\emph on
MEPC
\series default
\emph default
 register.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\series bold
\emph on
ECALL
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "ECALL"
description "Enviroment Call\\\\"
literal "true"

\end_inset


\series default
\emph default
 instruction is used to trigger a system call or exception, typically for
 transitioning control to the operating system or firmware, such as entering
 a handler in machine mode.
 Since it causes an exception, all instructions before the ECALL must complete
 before it is processed.
 The instruction is recognized during the Decode stage, but it will only
 proceed if no prior branch is taken—if a branch is taken, the Decode stage
 is flushed, and the ECALL will not execute.
 If a branch is not taken, the PC is loaded with the 
\emph on
mtvec
\emph default
 value and the decode and execute unit's are flushed and NOP is inserted.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\emph on
MRET
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "MRET"
description "Machine Return Instruction"
literal "true"

\end_inset


\series default
\emph default
 Instruction is used to return from an interrupt routine.
 PC is loaded with the value of mepc and normal program execution resumes.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The CSR (Control and Status Register) block in a RISC-V processor plays
 a pivotal role in exception and interrupt handling by maintaining critical
 system-level information.
 One of the key registers is 
\emph on
csr_mstatus
\emph default
, which monitors the processor's current state, including the privilege
 level, interrupt enable settings, and context stack, ensuring a smooth
 transition during trap entry and return.
 The 
\emph on
csr_mepc
\emph default
 register records the program counter (PC) of the instruction that was executing
 at the time the exception occurred, allowing the processor to resume execution
 from the correct point after handling the trap.
 The 
\emph on
csr_mie
\emph default
 register manages the interrupt enable bits, determining which specific
 interrupts are allowed to trigger a trap, while the 
\emph on
csr_mip
\emph default
 register indicates pending interrupts that await service.
 Finally, the 
\emph on
csr_mtvec
\emph default
 register holds the base address for the trap vector, guiding the processor
 to the appropriate handler routine during exceptions or interrupts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Exception Handling
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When entering an interrupt/exception handler, the core sets the 
\emph on
mepc
\emph default
 CSR to the current program counter and saves 
\emph on
mstatus.MIE
\emph default
 to 
\emph on
mstatus.MPIE
\emph default
.
 All exceptions cause the core to jump to the base address of the vector
 table in the 
\emph on
mtvec
\emph default
 CSR.
 Interrupts are handled in either non-vectored mode or vectored mode depending
 on the value of 
\emph on
mtvec.MODE
\emph default
.
 In non-vectored mode the core jumps to the base address of the vector table
 in the mtvec CSR.
 In vectored mode the core jumps to the base address plus four times the
 interrupt ID.
 Upon executing an mret instruction, the core jumps to the program counter
 previously saved in the 
\emph on
mepc
\emph default
 CSR and restores 
\emph on
mstatus.MPIE
\emph default
 to 
\emph on
mstatus.MIE.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\series bold
Exception Detection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Synchronous Exceptions: These are detected during the execution of instructions,
 such as illegal instructions, address misaligned, or access faults.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Asynchronous Exceptions: These include external interrupts, timer interrupts,
 and software interrupts.
 These are detected through external signals like 
\emph on
ext_irq
\emph default
, 
\emph on
timer_irq
\emph default
, and 
\emph on
sw_irq
\emph default
.
\end_layout

