\begin_layout Subsection
CSR Updates
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The below algorithm 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "alg:Exception-Entry-Sequence"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 shows how the CSR registers are updated when the core enters in the trap
 mode.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float algorithm
placement H
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
/* Synchronous Exception Entering Sequence */
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
1.
 mepc <- Current PC
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2.
 mcasue <- Specific cause code for that exception
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
3.
 mtval <- Value related to that exception
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
4.
 mstatus.mie <- 0 // Disable Global Interrupt Enable
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
5.
 mstatus.mip <- 1 // Pending Interrupt flag is raised 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
	PC <- mtvec_address 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Excepti
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "alg:Exception-Entry-Sequence"

\end_inset

on Entry Sequence.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
mepc
\series default

\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "mepc"
description "Machine Exception program counter"
literal "true"

\end_inset

: When a trap is taken into M-mode, 
\emph on
mepc
\emph default
 is written with the virtual address of the instruction that was interrupted
 or that encountered the exception.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
mcause
\series default

\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "mcause"
description "Machine Cause exception register"
literal "true"

\end_inset

: When a trap is taken into M-mode, 
\emph on
mcause
\emph default
 is written with a code indicating the event that caused the trap.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
mtval
\series default

\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "mtval"
description "Machine Trap value register"
literal "true"

\end_inset

: When a trap is taken into M-mode, 
\emph on
mtval
\emph default
 is either set to zero or written with exception-specific information to
 assist software in handling the trap.
 Eg.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
When a misaligned load or store operation results in an access-fault or
 page-fault exception, the 
\emph on
mtval
\emph default
 register is updated with a nonzero value that holds the virtual address
 corresponding to the part of the access that triggered the fault.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
When an instruction access-fault or page-fault exception occurs, then 
\emph on
mtval
\emph default
 will contain the virtual address of the of the instruction that caused
 the fault, while mepc will point to the beginning of the instruction.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
mstatus
\series default

\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "mstatus"
description "Machine status register"
literal "true"

\end_inset

: The 
\emph on
mstatus
\emph default
 register is updated to reflect the new state.
 For example, the MPIE bit is set to the current MIE state, and MIE is cleared.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
mtvec
\series default

\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "mtvec"
description "Machine trap vector address"
literal "true"

\end_inset

: The 
\emph on
mtvec
\emph default
 register is used to determine the base address for the trap vector.
 It has a direct mode and a vectored mode.
 In direct mode it always points to the base address of the vector table.
 In vectored mode, the mtvec registers points to the base_address + offset,
 determined by the mcause code of the interrupt that occured.
 
\end_layout

