\begin_layout Standard
The following CSRs are essential for managing interrupts and exceptions
 in the RISC-V architecture: 
\emph on
mstatus
\emph default
 (0x300) holds the machine status, including global interrupt enable and
 privilege mode information; 
\emph on
mie
\emph default
 (0x304) allows enabling of specific interrupts; 
\emph on
mtvec
\emph default
 (0x305) contains the base address for the trap handler; 
\emph on
mepc
\emph default
 (0x341) stores the program counter at the time of an exception; 
\emph on
mcause
\emph default
 (0x342) indicates the cause of the trap; 
\emph on
mtval
\emph default
 (0x343) provides fault-specific data like the address or instruction that
 caused the exception; and 
\emph on
mip
\emph default
 (0x344) reflects which interrupts are currently pending.
 These CSRs are initialized by software immediately after the processor
 comes out of reset to configure the core’s behavior in response to traps
 and interrupts.
\end_layout

