\begin_layout Standard
Attempting to fetch an instruction from a page that does not have execute
 permissions raises a fetch 
\series bold
page-fault exception
\series default
.
 Attempting to execute a load or load-reserved instruction whose effective
 address lies within a page without read permissions raises a 
\series bold
load page-fault exception
\series default
.
 Attempting to execute a store, store-conditional, or AMO instruction whose
 effective address lies within a page without write permissions raises a
 
\series bold
store page-fault exception.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Page Table Walker
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The two-level page table walker is a hardware mechanism that automates the
 translation process.
 When we get VPN
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "VPN"
description "Virtual Page Number\\\\"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 (virtual page number) and translation request from the processor, corresponding
 translation is looked at the CAM (Content addresable memory), if there
 exsist a previous matching then the PPN
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "PPN"
description "Physical Page Number\\\\"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 is given out along with the 
\series bold
tlb_hit
\series default
 signal.
 If we encounter the miss, then the mapping needs to looked in the page
 table located in the memory.
 Hence we need to do the page table walk.
 Page table walker is shown in the figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Page-Table-Walker"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename /home/rclab/Documents/MMU/MMU_FSM.pdf
	scale 55

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Page Tabl
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:Page-Table-Walker"

\end_inset

e Walker.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The translation process involves two stages, each corresponding to a level
 in the page table hierarchy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Level 1 Translation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Base Address Calculation: The base address of the level 1 page table is
 obtained from the satp register.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
satp.PPN
\emph default
 provides the base address of the level 1 page table.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The base address is shifted left by 12 bits to align with the page table
 entry size (4 KiB).
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Index Calculation: The index into the level 1 page table is derived from
 
\emph on
vpn1
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
vpn1[9:0] is used to index into the level 1 page table.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The address of the level 1 page table entry (PTE1) is calculated as: 
\series bold
pte1_address
\series default
 = satp.PPN << 12 | vpn1[9:0] << 2
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
PTE1 Access: The level 1 page table entry (PTE1) is accessed at the calculated
 address.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
PTE1 contains the base address of the corresponding level 2 page table.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Level 2 Translation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Base Address Calculation: The base address of the level 2 page table is
 obtained from PTE1 if that is not the leaf entry.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The base address is shifted left by 12 bits to align with the page table
 entry size (4 KiB).
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Index Calculation: The index into the level 2 page table is derived from
 
\emph on
vpn0
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
vpn0[9:0] is used to index into the level 2 page table.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The address of the level 2 page table entry (PTE2) is calculated as: 
\series bold
pte2_address
\series default
 = pte1.PPN << 12 | vpn0[9:0] << 2
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
PTE2 Access: The level 2 page table entry (PTE2) is accessed at the calculated
 address.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
PTE2 contains the physical page number (PPN) and other metadata (e.g., permissions
, accessed bit, dirty bit).
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Final Physical Address Calculation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
PPN Extraction: The PPN is extracted from PTE2.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The PPN is combined with the offset to form the final physical address.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The final physical address is calculated as: 
\series bold
physical_address
\series default
 = pte2.PPN << 12 | offset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If either PTE1 or PTE2 is invalid (i.e., the V bit is 0), a page fault exception
 is raised.
 The exception handler can then take appropriate action, such as loading
 the missing page table entry or handling the fault.
 The permissions and access control are checked during the translation process:
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection
VIPT cache
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since translation process requires fetching of page from the main memory
 and sometimes from DRAM (in case of OS), it can slow down the process of
 memory access.
 Hence to avoid that, we are using VIPT caches both for instruction and
 data memory.
 In VIPT caches, the index and block offset bits (12-bits in our case) are
 used to fetch cache line and parallely virtual_tag is given to the TLB
 for physical mapping (or physical tag).
 Once we get this 
\emph on
physical_tag
\emph default
, it is compared against the 
\emph on
tag_out
\emph default
 from the cache memory to check if it's a hit or miss.
 The figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:VIPT-cache-design"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 shows how the VIPT caches are instantiated.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename /home/rclab/Documents/MMU/MMU_VIPT_Cache.pdf
	scale 120

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
VIPT cach
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:VIPT-cache-design"

\end_inset

e design.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
VIPT (Virtually Indexed, Physically Tagged) caches are a type of cache memory
 organization that combines the benefits of both virtual and physical caching
 strategies.
 In a VIPT cache, the cache is indexed using the virtual address, which
 allows for faster cache access since the translation from virtual to physical
 address is not required at cache lookup time.
 This can significantly reduce the latency associated with cache accesses,
 especially in systems where address translation is complex or involves
 multiple stages, such as in virtualized environments or systems with large
 page tables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The cache lines in a VIPT cache are tagged with the physical address, which
 ensures that the cache coherence and consistency are maintained across
 different processes and address spaces.
 This tagging mechanism helps in avoiding conflicts and false sharing between
 different virtual addresses that may map to the same physical address,
 thereby improving the overall cache performance and reducing cache thrashing.
