\begin_layout Standard
At system startup, the debugger initiates a structured initialization sequence
 to establish communication and prepare the target for debug operations.
 The process begins by setting up a USB 2.0 Full-Speed virtual COM port (
\emph on
VCP
\emph default
) interface between the host machine and the STM32F407-based USB-to-JTAG
 translator.
 This link serves as the communication backbone for transmitting JTAG signals
 and debug commands.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Once the USB connection is successfully established, the STM32F407 configures
 its GPIO pins for JTAG signal output 
\emph on
(TCK, TMS, TDI, TDO)
\emph default
 and performs a hardware reset of the Test Access Port (
\emph on
TAP
\emph default
) controller.
 The TAP is transitioned from an unknown state to the 
\emph on
Test-Logic-Reset
\emph default
 state by issuing a sequence of five consecutive logic '1' bits on the 
\emph on
TMS
\emph default
 line, as per the IEEE 1149.1 JTAG standard.
 Subsequently, the TAP enters the 
\emph on
Run-Test/Idle
\emph default
 state, ready for further JTAG operations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Following TAP initialization, the debugger verifies the readiness of the
 
\emph on
Debug Transport Module (DTM)
\emph default
 and the 
\emph on
Debug Module Interface (DMI)
\emph default
 by polling the 
\emph on
dtmcs
\emph default
 (DTM control and status) and 
\emph on
dmstatus
\emph default
 (Debug Module status) registers, respectively.
 These checks ensure that the debug infrastructure is responsive and functional.
 The 
\emph on
IDCODE
\emph default
 is then read and validated to confirm the identity of the target device.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Once all checks pass, the system is ready to receive debug commands from
 the user.
 This entire process is depicted in the system initialization flow diagram
 shown in figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Debugger Initialization steps"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Instruction Execution Control
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Execution control is a fundamental capability of the debugger.
 It allows halting the processor, inspecting or modifying state, and resuming
 execution.
 These features are implemented through write operations to the 
\emph on
dmcontrol
\emph default
 register.
 Halting the core is achieved by asserting the 
\emph on
haltreq
\emph default
 bit, while resumption is triggered by setting the 
\emph on
resumereq
\emph default
 bit.
 Single-stepping is supported by setting the 
\emph on
step
\emph default
 bit in the 
\emph on
dcsr
\emph default
 register, which instructs the core to execute exactly one instruction after
 being resumed.
 These operations rely on a clean handshake protocol between the Debug Module
 and the core's debug interface.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
State Monitoring
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To monitor the processor state, the debugger reads internal registers via
 
\emph on
abstract
\emph default
 commands.
 These include:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
GPRs
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
CSRs
\emph default
, accessed using Access Register commands
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Memory locations, accessed using Access Memory commands
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The results of such operations are routed through the 
\emph on
data0
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
data1
\emph default
 registers, which temporarily store the data during DMI transactions.
 Additionally, core status is obtained from the 
\emph on
dmstatus
\emph default
 register, which indicates whether the core is halted, running, or reset.
 The 
\emph on
abstractcs
\emph default
 register provides status information about the abstract command engine,
 including error flags (
\emph on
cmderr
\emph default
) and whether the engine is currently busy.
 
\end_layout

