\begin_layout Subsection
Debug Module
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename /home/rclab/Downloads/Thesis_Report/Debugger/DEBUG_overview.png
	lyxscale 2
	scale 3.3

\end_inset


\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:Debuger Module Architecture"

\end_inset

Debuger Module Architecture.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Debug Module (DM) is the principal logic block responsible for managing
 external debugging interactions with the RISC-V processor core.
 It serves as the terminal endpoint for Debug Module Interface (DMI) transaction
s and mediates all commands from the host debugger to the internal debug
 infrastructure.
 This includes operations such as halting and resuming the core, reading/writing
 general-purpose and control/status registers (
\emph on
GPRs
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
CSRs
\emph default
), memory inspection and modification, and runtime monitoring of processor
 state.
 The DM design adheres to the RISC-V Debug Specification v1.0 with multiple
 custom extensions to support self-verification and deep observability for
 validation purposes, as shown in the figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Debuger Module Architecture"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Architecture and Role in the Debug Flow
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At a high level, the Debug Module (
\emph on
DM
\emph default
) functions as an intermediary between the Debug Transport Module (
\emph on
DTM
\emph default
) and the RISC-V core and memory subsystem.
 The 
\emph on
DTM
\emph default
 receives serialized commands from the external debugger via the JTAG interface
 and translates these into simple register read/write operations.
 The 
\emph on
DMI
\emph default
 (Debug Module Interface) facilitates communication between the 
\emph on
DTM
\emph default
 and the 
\emph on
DM
\emph default
 registers, enabling the transfer of data.
 Based on the contents of the command register, the 
\emph on
DM
\emph default
 performs abstract operations such as register or memory access.
 These operations often involve the 
\emph on
data0
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
data1
\emph default
 registers, which temporarily hold operand values and results for these
 operations, allowing for seamless execution of debugging tasks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Implemented Debug Module Registers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following registers form the core of the debug module:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
dmcontrol
\emph default
 
\emph on
(0x10):
\series default
\emph default
 A 32-bit register for managing the DM and harts.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
dmactive: 
\emph default
Master enable for the DM.
 Must be set to 1 for other fields to be active.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
ndmreset:
\emph default
 Optional field to reset the core or other non-debug elements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
haltreq:
\emph default
 Request to halt the selected hart.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
resumereq:
\emph default
 Request to resume execution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
hartsel:
\emph default
 Selects which hart is targeted for debug operations.
 Only a single-hart system is used in this implementation.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
dmstatus (0x11):
\emph default
 
\series default
Reports the status of the DM and selected hart.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
allhalted
\emph default
, 
\emph on
anyhalted:
\emph default
 Indicate the halt status of harts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
allrunning
\emph default
, 
\emph on
anyrunning:
\emph default
 Indicate running status.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
authenticated: 
\emph default
Always set to 1 as authentication is not implemented.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
version:
\emph default
 Set to 2, as per RISC-V Debug Spec v1.0.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
abstractcs (0x16):
\series default
\emph default
 Reports the state of the abstract command engine.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
busy:
\emph default
 Indicates whether an abstract command is currently in progress.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
cmderr:
\emph default
 3-bit error code; cleared on write of 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
datacount:
\emph default
 Set to 2, representing availability of 
\emph on
data0
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
data1
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
progbufsize:
\emph default
 Set to 0, as no 
\emph on
program buffer
\emph default
 is implemented.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
support:
\emph default
 Reflects that only 
\emph on
Access Register
\emph default
 (0) and 
\emph on
Access Memory
\emph default
 (2) are supported.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
command (0x17):
\emph default
 
\series default
Writing to this register initiates an abstract command.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Supported commands:
\end_layout

