\begin_layout Subsection
Reset Control and Debug Entry Coordination
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Reset control is a critical feature in the debug architecture, enabling
 the debugger to reliably manage the processor’s execution lifecycle and
 gain visibility from the very first instruction after reset.
 The implemented debug system supports two complementary reset mechanisms—one
 initiated via the Debug Module and the other through an external hardware
 switch—each integrated to ensure flexibility, safety, and effective debug
 entry.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Debug Module-based reset is triggered by the external debugger through
 the 
\emph on
dmcontrol
\emph default
 register.
 This software-controlled reset signal is internally synchronized and routed
 to the processor core and associated debug logic.
 Upon release of the 
\emph on
reset
\emph default
, the Debug Module can assert a 
\emph on
haltreq
\emph default
 signal, allowing the processor to enter debug mode immediately after reset.
 This supports the RISC-V Debug Specification's requirement for "
\emph on
debug-after-reset
\emph default
" behavior.
 The mechanism ensures that the debugger can observe and intervene before
 the processor executes the first instruction post-reset, enabling use cases
 such as boot-time diagnostics, breakpoint setup, or register probing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Parallel to this, the system includes a manual reset mechanism via a physical
 push-button switch that triggers a global hardware reset.
 This reset line synchronously resets all major subsystems, including the
 core, memory interface, and debug infrastructure.
 While this method ensures a clean and full system reinitialization, it
 requires the debugger to re-establish the debug connection and reconfigure
 debug state (e.g., reasserting halt requests or restoring context).
 To distinguish between these reset causes, the debug logic inspects reset
 signals and can maintain internal state only when reset is initiated via
 the debug interface.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In both scenarios, reset coordination is handled carefully to avoid metastabilit
y or inconsistent states.
 All resets are synchronized with system clocks, and transitions between
 reset and active states are controlled via explicit state machines.
 The implementation ensures that TAP controller integrity is maintained
 across reset cycles, and all required Debug Module registers are reinitialized
 or retained appropriately depending on the reset source.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This dual reset mechanism, combining controlled debugger-triggered reset
 with full-system hardware reset, provides comprehensive control for development
, testing, and fault recovery, making the debug system robust and responsive
 to diverse use cases.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Run Control
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Run control is the core mechanism through which the external debugger can
 orchestrate and manage the execution state of the processor.
 It encompasses halting and resuming the core, initiating single-step execution,
 and coordinating transitions between normal and debug modes.
 In compliance with the RISC-V Debug Specification, and tailored to a single-har
t implementation, the debug module provides fine-grained control over the
 processor's execution state through a combination of hardware signals and
 abstract commands.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The primary mechanism for halting the processor is the assertion of the
 haltreq bit within the 
\emph on
dmcontrol
\emph default
 register.
 When this bit is set by the external debugger, the Debug Module asserts
 an internal halt request signal to the core.
 The core responds by entering debug mode at a well-defined point in execution
 and sets the 
\emph on
allhalted
\emph default
 bit in the 
\emph on
dmstatus
\emph default
 register, which serves as confirmation to the debugger that the halt was
 successful.
 The halted state allows the debugger to read or write general-purpose registers
 (
\emph on
GPRs
\emph default
), control and status registers (
\emph on
CSRs
\emph default
), and initiate memory transactions through the abstract command interface.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Resuming execution is initiated by setting the 
\emph on
resumereq
\emph default
 bit in the 
\emph on
dmcontrol
\emph default
 register.
 Upon detecting this request, the Debug Module clears the 
\emph on
haltreq
\emph default
 and generates a 
\emph on
resume
\emph default
 signal to the core.
 The core then exits debug mode and resumes normal execution from the address
 stored in the 
\emph on
dpc
\emph default
 
\emph on
(Debug Program Counter)
\emph default
 register.
 The 
\emph on
allrunning
\emph default
 bit in 
\emph on
dmstatus
\emph default
 is used to acknowledge that the core has resumed successfully.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In addition to full halt/resume control, the system supports hardware single-ste
p execution.
 When the 
\emph on
step
\emph default
 bit in the 
\emph on
dcsr
\emph default
 
\emph on
(Debug Control and Status Register)
\emph default
 is set, and the processor is resumed from debug mode, it executes exactly
 one instruction and automatically re-enters debug mode.
 This behavior allows for precise instruction-level debugging without external
 breakpoints or complex instrumentation.
 The debugger can read the updated 
\emph on
dpc
\emph default
 after each step to trace program flow in a fine-grained manner.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The core’s entry into debug mode can also be triggered via exceptions or
 external interrupts, depending on the configuration in the 
\emph on
dcsr
\emph default
.
 However, in this implementation, debug entry is typically coordinated via
 explicit requests from the debugger to ensure deterministic behavior and
 full external control.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All control signals between the Debug Module and the core are synchronized
 and safely latched to avoid glitches or metastable conditions.
 These include 
\emph on
haltreq
\emph default
, 
\emph on
resumereq
\emph default
, 
\emph on
ack_halted
\emph default
, 
\emph on
ack_running
\emph default
, and 
\emph on
single_step
\emph default
.
 Custom logic ensures that only one control path dominates at any time,
 preventing conflicts in state transitions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This robust run-control infrastructure ensures reliable interaction between
 the debugger and the processor, enabling essential debug capabilities such
 as program inspection, register probing, and step-by-step execution control.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Abstract Command Execution and Data Register Handling
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The RISC-V Debug Module implements abstract commands to allow an external
 debugger to perform basic operations such as reading/writing general-purpose
 or control/status registers, and accessing memory.
 These commands are encoded in the 
\emph on
command
\emph default
 register and executed when the core is in debug halt mode.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
command
\emph default
 register is a 32-bit register that encodes the command type and associated
 control fields.
 Two types of abstract commands have been implemented in this design: Access
 Register (
\emph on
cmdtype
\emph default
 = 0x00) and Access Memory (
\emph on
cmdtype
\emph default
 = 0x02).
 Execution of a command may involve one or both of the Data Registers, 
\emph on
data0
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
data1
\emph default
, to pass arguments and results.
\end_layout

