\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Operational Flow:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The base memory address is supplied in 
\emph on
data1
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If 
\emph on
write
\emph default
 is set, the content in 
\emph on
data0
\emph default
 is written to the memory location.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If 
\emph on
write
\emph default
 is clear, the value at the memory location is loaded into 
\emph on
data0
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
aampostincrement
\emph default
, if set, increments the memory address in 
\emph on
data1
\emph default
 post-access.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The 
\emph on
aamsize
\emph default
 field determines the access granularity (byte/half-word/word).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Only physical addresses are supported (
\emph on
aamvirtual
\emph default
 = 0).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\series bold
Data Registers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two data registers are implemented:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
data0:
\emph default
 The primary register for passing and receiving data during abstract command
 execution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
data1:
\emph default
 Used primarily for memory commands, representing the memory address (or
 secondary data in multi-operand instructions).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Data registers serve as operand channels between the debugger and the debug
 module.
 Their contents are valid only while the core is halted.
 Care is taken to ensure all abstract command execution completes before
 resuming the core to avoid data corruption.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Debug Module Registers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Debug Module (DM) implements a set of memory-mapped registers through
 which an external debugger can manage and monitor core activity.
 These registers conform to the RISC-V Debug Specification and are accessed
 via the Debug Module Interface (DMI).
 The implemented registers allow core control, status reporting, abstract
 command execution, and customized debugging workflows.
 All registers are addressable via the DMI address space, with specific
 control and status bits determining the flow of debug interactions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\series bold
dmcontrol (Address:0x10):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
dmcontrol
\emph default
 register is the principal control mechanism for managing core-level debug
 operations via the Debug Module Interface (DMI).
 This 32-bit register enables halt/resume requests, hart selection, and
 system/hart-level resets.
 All interactions between the debugger and the core are mediated through
 this register, making it critical to the functionality of the external
 debug interface, as detailed in table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Bit-fields-and-5"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

