\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Design Considerations and Implementation Notes:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Activation: All debug control activity is gated by the 
\emph on
dmactive
\emph default
 bit.
 This must be set before any requests such as 
\emph on
halt
\emph default
, 
\emph on
resume
\emph default
, or 
\emph on
reset
\emph default
 take effect.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Reset Handling: 
\series default
Two reset pathways are supported: one via 
\emph on
ndmreset
\emph default
 (for full system reset without affecting the debug module), and another
 via 
\emph on
hartreset
\emph default
 (for selective hart-level reset).
 Both are implemented in coordination with an external physical reset switch
 for complete board-level reinitialization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Halt on Reset:
\series default
 The combination of 
\emph on
setresethaltreq
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
clrresethaltreq
\emph default
 enables or disables the ability to force a core to halt immediately after
 a reset—critical for debugging from the first instruction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Single-Hart Operation:
\series default
 Although this implementation targets a single-hart processor, the hart
 selection fields (
\emph on
hartselhi
\emph default
, 
\emph on
hartsello
\emph default
, 
\emph on
hasel
\emph default
) are retained for compliance with the RISC-V Debug Specification and future
 scalability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
No Keepalive Fields:
\series default
 The 
\emph on
setkeepalive
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
clrkeepalive
\emph default
 fields described in the specification are not implemented, as no timeout
 or heartbeat mechanism is currently required in the system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\series bold
dmstatus (Address: 0x11): 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
dmstatus
\emph default
 register is a 32-bit read-only status register that reflects the real-time
 operational state of the Debug Module and the associated hart(s).
 It provides essential insight into the availability, running/halted state,
 and reset status of the core, as well as metadata about debug infrastructure
 implementation.
 This register is queried regularly by the external debugger to synchronize
 with the core's debug state, as summarized in table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Bit-fields-and-2"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

