\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Execution Semantics:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Command Lifecycle:
\series default
 A command is written to the 
\emph on
command
\emph default
 register, and its associated operands (such as register or memory address)
 are placed in 
\emph on
data0
\emph default
 and/or 
\emph on
data1
\emph default
.
 The command execution begins immediately and sets the 
\emph on
abstractcs.busy
\emph default
 bit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Completion and Error Reporting: 
\series default
Upon completion, the 
\emph on
busy
\emph default
 bit is cleared and the result is indicated in 
\emph on
abstractcs.cmderr
\emph default
.
 If a command fails due to an invalid regno, unsupported size, or bad cmdtype,
 appropriate error codes are latched.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Post-Increment: 
\series default
This feature allows automated iteration through consecutive registers or
 memory addresses, simplifying multi-word transactions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\series bold
data0, data1 (Addresses: 0x04–0x05): 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
data0
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
data1
\emph default
 registers are 32-bit wide memory-mapped registers used as the primary data
 interface between the debugger and the target core through the debug module.
 These registers are part of the abstract command mechanism and are used
 for both register and memory access operations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Purpose and Usage (see 
\series default

\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Description-of-dataN"

\end_inset


\series bold
):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
data0:
\emph default
 
\series default
This is the primary data register used for transferring values to/from the
 RISC-V core or memory.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
data1:
\emph default
 
\series default
This auxiliary register is primarily used to hold memory addresses during
 abstract memory access commands.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These registers are critical for read and write transactions initiated through
 the command register.
 Their content is interpreted based on the cmdtype field in the command,
 which determines whether the access is targeted at a core register or memory.
\end_layout

