\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Implementation Notes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Both registers are implemented with simple latching mechanisms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
They are not cleared after command execution and retain their last value
 until overwritten.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
No locking mechanism is enforced; coherence is ensured by command flow control
 and the busy signal in abstractcs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\series bold
Custom Registers (custom0 to custom6) (Addresses: 0x70–0x76): 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To support staged bring-up and validation of the debugger independently
 of the RISC-V core, a set of custom debug registers (
\emph on
custom0
\emph default
 through 
\emph on
custom6
\emph default
) has been integrated.
 These registers implement two essential mechanisms:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Bypass: 
\series default
Simulates responses from the RISC-V core for abstract commands, enabling
 the debugger to function even in the absence of a running core.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Probing:
\series default
 Observes and captures signal values as they arrive at the Debug Module
 from the core, without interfering with normal operation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Both mechanisms operate simultaneously, ensuring that while the debugger
 can function using simulated inputs, the actual signal paths from the core
 can still be verified for correctness and consistency (refer to table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Description-of-the"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

