\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Simultaneous Bypass and Probing:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The design allows bypass mode (enabled via 
\emph on
custom3
\emph default
) and signal probing to operate in parallel:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
When the debugger is in bypass mode, it relies entirely on values from 
\emph on
custom0
\emph default
, 
\emph on
custom1
\emph default
, and 
\emph on
custom2
\emph default
 to respond to abstract commands.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
At the same time, 
\emph on
custom4
\emph default
, 
\emph on
custom5
\emph default
, and 
\emph on
custom6
\emph default
 do not interfere with signal flow but passively record what the core would
 have sent to the DM, allowing for diagnostic and verification use.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This design ensures:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Debugger development and testing can proceed independently of core integration.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Real-time visibility into core-DM interactions, improving traceability and
 observability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Bypass logic does not suppress or overwrite actual core signals; both views
 coexist for comprehensive testing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Debug Core integration
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Debug Mode Entry
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When the debugger requests for the debug entry, the processor enters in
 the debug mode.
 Currently we have three main debug entry points.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
\emph on
Haltreq
\emph default
 goes high (Async debug entry): 
\series default
If the processor is in normal operation (pipeline operation) and it detects
 the 
\emph on
haltreq
\emph default
 signal, then we also enter the Debug state if the processor is not freezed.
 As soon as we enter the debug mode, 
\emph on
pending_async_dbg
\emph default
 goes low.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\emph on
resethaltreq
\emph default
 goes high (Reset debug entry)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Single Stepping
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
EBREAK (Sync debug entry)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Debug Mode is a special processor mode used only when a hart is halted for
 external debugging.
 Because the hart is halted, there is no forward progress in the normal
 instruction stream.
 How Debug Mode is implemented is not specified here.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Following things happen during debug mode entry: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Kills all the instructions in the pipeline.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
All interrupts are masked using 
\emph on
interrupt_dbg_global
\emph default
 signal and also by writing mstatus[MIE] = 1'b0 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
All traps are disabled
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
All the debug instructions are executed as they do in M-mode except control
 instructions (
\series bold
Branch and Jumps
\series default
) irrespective of their destination address, all PC related instructions
 like 
\series bold
AUIPC 
\series default
act as illegal istructions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
RESET
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the 
\emph on
halt
\emph default
 signal (driven by the hart’s halt request bit in the Debug Module) or 
\emph on
hasresethaltreq
\emph default
 are asserted when a hart comes out of reset, the hart must enter Debug
 Mode before executing any instructions, but after performing any initialization
 that would usually happen before the first instruction is executed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
HALT
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before entering the debug mode, the processor is halted when it encounters
 any of the debug entry indication through CSR or through external signals.
 In HALT mode, we do following things:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\emph on
cause
\emph default
 is updates with the cause of debug
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\emph on
prv
\emph default
 is set to 3 and 
\emph on
v
\emph default
 is set to 0 to indicate Machine Mode.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\emph on
dpc
\emph default
 is set to the next valid instruction which needs to be executed when the
 core leaves the debug mode.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Kill all the instructions in the pipeline
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
write mstatus[MIE] <= 1'b0, to disable all the Software, timer and external
 interrupts.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Assert 
\emph on
interrupt_dbg_global 
\emph default
signal 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
PC <- dm_halt_addr (Program buffer)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When the processor is halted, it waits for signal from debugger that whether
 it has to execute the abstract command or start the execution from the
 program buffer.
 If processor wants to single step the instruction, then the debugger has
 to write the DCSR[step] = 1'b1 using the abstract command and then it can
 go in the single stepping mode.
 If processor recevies the 
\emph on
resumereq_i
\emph default
, then it goes back to the functional (normal runnning) mode.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If while executing the program buffer EBREAK occurs, then the PC goes to
 the starting of program buffer and the processor is again halted.
 All the interrupts are disbaled in the HALT mode.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
DEBUG EXIT SEQUENCE
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When we get 
\emph on
resumereq_i
\emph default
 as 1'b1, following sequence is followed.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Fetch PC is retored with the one from 
\emph on
dpc
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Previllage levels are restores as it was before entering the debug mode.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Leave the debug mode.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Single Steping
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This method is only available to external debuggers, and is the preferred
 way to single step.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If the external debugger sets the 
\series bold
\emph on
step
\series default
\emph default
 bit before resuming a halted hart, the processor will execute exactly one
 instruction before automatically re-entering Debug Mode.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If a trap (e.g., exception or interrupt) occurs while executing the instruction,
 the processor will enter Debug Mode right after the PC is updated to the
 trap handler address.
 The 
\emph on
mtval
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
mcause
\emph default
 CSRs will be updated however, the trap handler is not executed; it’s immediatel
y halted again.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If the executed instruction changes the PC to a location that would cause
 an exception (e.g., instruction fetch fault), that exception is deferred
 and will only be taken next time the hart resumes.
\end_layout

