\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\emph on
FUNCTIONAL State
\series default
\emph default
: Upon coming out of reset, the processor checks the 
\emph on
haltreq_i
\emph default
 signal.
 If this signal is not asserted, the processor enters the FUNCTIONAL state.
 In this state, the processor behaves normally—fetching instructions from
 instruction memory, executing them through the pipeline, and writing results
 back to the register file or memory.
 Entry into debug mode is not permitted while the processor is stalled,
 such as during memory latency (ICACHE/DCACHE) or FPU operations.
 Even if debug requests such as 
\emph on
ndmreset
\emph default
 or 
\emph on
haltreq_i
\emph default
 are asserted during this time, the FSM defers action until the processor
 becomes idle and resumes normal flow.
 This behavior ensures that the processor only transitions into debug when
 it is safe to do so, preserving pipeline integrity and consistency.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\emph on
DBG_RESET_REQ State:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If a non-debug module reset (
\emph on
ndmreset
\emph default
) is received while in the FUNCTIONAL state, the FSM transitions to the
 DBG_RESET_REQ state.
 In this state, the processor remains in reset, in accordance with the debug
 spec, until the 
\emph on
ndmreset
\emph default
 signal is deasserted.
 Once the reset is lifted, the FSM checks the 
\emph on
resethaltreq_i 
\emph default
signal.
 If this is asserted, indicating a request to enter debug mode immediately
 after reset, the FSM transitions to the DBG_TAKEN state.
 Otherwise, it stays in DBG_RESET_REQ or returns to FUNCTIONAL depending
 on other pending conditions.
 This mechanism allows a debugger to take control of the processor directly
 out of reset.
 
\series bold
\emph on
DBG_TAKEN State: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The DBG_TAKEN state handles the initiation of a debug session.
 The FSM transitions here if 
\emph on
haltreq_i
\emph default
 is asserted or if an appropriate debug entry condition is detected (e.g.,
 from a triggered breakpoint, step event, or ebreak instruction).
 In this state, the cause of the debug event is written into the dcsr.cause
 CSR as defined in the RISC-V Debug Specification.
 If the debug cause is not due to single-step execution, the currently executing
 program counter is stored in the 
\emph on
dpc
\emph default
 register.
 The processor’s PC is then loaded with the 
\emph on
dm_halt_address
\emph default
, which points to the beginning of the debug program buffer, and the 
\emph on
debug_mode
\emph default
 signal is asserted.
 From here, the FSM transitions into the DBG_MODE state, indicating that
 the processor is now fully in debug mode and halted for external inspection
 or instruction.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\emph on
DBG_MODE State: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the DBG_MODE state, the processor is halted and remains idle, waiting
 for instructions from the debug host.
 It allows the debugger to inspect and modify state or initiate specific
 debug actions.
 If the debug module receives a 
\emph on
new_cmd_flag
\emph default
, the FSM transitions to the ABSTRACT_COMMAND state to execute abstract
 commands, which may include register access or memory operations.
 Alternatively, if the 
\emph on
pbuffer_execution 
\emph default
signal is asserted, the FSM enters the PROGRAM_BUFFER state to begin executing
 a sequence of instructions from the program buffer.
 The FSM stays in DBG_MODE as long as no execution requests are pending,
 providing a safe and passive state for debug interaction.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\emph on
ABSTRACT_COMMAND State: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The ABSTRACT_COMMAND state manages the execution of abstract commands issued
 via the debug module interface (DMI).
 These commands are typically used to read/write registers or access memory.
 The processor executes these commands one at a time through the instruction
 pipeline.
 Once a command is successfully executed, the FSM sets the command_complete
 flag and returns to DBG_MODE to await the next command.
 If a resume request (
\emph on
resumereq_i
\emph default
) is detected during or after command execution, the FSM prepares to exit
 debug mode and transitions to the DBG_EXIT state.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\emph on
PROGRAM_BUFFER State:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the PROGRAM_BUFFER state, the processor begins executing instructions
 from a predefined buffer area (
\emph on
dm_halt_address
\emph default
) loaded during the DBG_TAKEN state.
 This mode is used for running short debug programs, such as test sequences
 or small memory routines.
 Execution continues until an EBREAK instruction is encountered, which serves
 as an exit condition.
 Upon reaching EBREAK, the FSM transitions back to DBG_MODE to await further
 commands.
 Similar to the ABSTRACT_COMMAND state, if 
\emph on
resumereq_i
\emph default
 is asserted during program buffer execution, the FSM will instead transition
 to DBG_EXIT.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\emph on
DBG_EXIT State: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The DBG_EXIT state handles the safe exit from debug mode.
 Here, the PC is restored with the value saved in the 
\emph on
dpc
\emph default
 register, effectively resuming execution from the instruction where the
 core had been halted.
 The 
\emph on
debug_mode
\emph default
 signal is deasserted, and the FSM returns to the FUNCTIONAL state, resuming
 normal operation.
 This transition ensures that any modifications made during debug mode are
 correctly applied, and program control is seamlessly handed back to the
 running application.
\end_layout

